5. Discussion 

The present study was designed to characterize the deep binding pocket (DBP) of the MexB multidrug efflux transporter and to identify ligands capable of occupying this region through a structure-based computational workflow (Satoshi  et al.,2006). The analysis progressed from structural characterization of MexB to conformational-state comparison, binding-pocket prediction, virtual screening, docking, and interaction-based validation using experimentally characterized reference ligands. 

5.1 Structural characterization of the MexB binding pocket 

The MexB structure used in this study was analyzed as a trimeric RND transporter containing three conformationally distinct protomers corresponding to the Access, Binding, and Extrusion states (Akihito et al., 2025). Structural superposition of the transmembrane domains showed that the major conformational differences occur primarily in the porter domain rather than being attributable to large changes in the transmembrane region. This supports the use of the Binding-state protomer for detailed ligand-binding analysis. 

The Binding-state porter domain was selected for subsequent DBP characterization because this state represents the conformation associated with ligand recognition and binding within the porter domain. P2Rank analysis subsequently identified candidate ligand-binding regions, allowing the distal/deep binding region to be investigated computationally (Radoslav  et al., 2018). 

5.2 Identification of the core DBP residues 

Detailed structural analysis identified six residues forming the core region used for subsequent ligand-interaction analysis: 

PHE178  

VAL139  

TYR327  

PHE573  

PHE610  

PHE628  

The predominance of phenylalanine residues is consistent with the aromatic character of the MexB distal binding region described in previous structural studies(Ryosuke  et al.,2013). These residues provide a combination of hydrophobic and aromatic environments that can accommodate chemically diverse ligand structures. 

The distance analysis of the five top-ranked predicted ligands showed that all five ligands had a heavy-atom contact within 4 Å of each of the six core residues. Thus, each of the selected predicted ligands demonstrated a 6/6 core-residue contact pattern under the distance criterion used in this study (Jerome et al., 2021). 

The minimum distances were generally approximately 3.15–3.75 Å. These distances are consistent with close ligand–residue proximity and support the interpretation that the selected ligands occupy the defined DBP region rather than merely docking elsewhere within the large search box. 

