CHEMBL254328 produced the most negative Vina score among the five selected compounds, at −12.90 kcal/mol, and simultaneously maintained contacts with all six core residues. Its minimum distance to PHE178 was approximately 3.15 Å, representing the shortest PHE178 contact among the five predicted ligands (Jerome et al., 2021). 

CHEMBL1170, CHEMBL1200374, CHEMBL2107826 and CHEMBL1336 similarly maintained contacts across the complete six-residue core. This consistency is important because it indicates that the selection was not based solely on docking score; the ligands also reproduced the spatial occupation of the defined DBP. 

However, the Vina scores should be interpreted as computational docking scores, not experimentally measured binding free energies. Differences between docking scores should therefore be considered within the limitations of the docking method (Jerome et al., 2021). 

5.4 Nature of ligand–residue interactions 

The geometry analysis classified most interactions involving PHE178, PHE573, PHE610, PHE628 and TYR327 as aromatic-contact candidates. VAL139 was generally classified as a close-contact residue. 

For example, CHEMBL254328 showed minimum distances of: 

PHE178 — 3.154 Å  

VAL139 — 3.487 Å  

TYR327 — 3.566 Å  

PHE573 — 3.731 Å  

PHE610 — 3.705 Å  

PHE628 — 3.429 Å  

This pattern indicates that CHEMBL254328 occupies the central region defined by the six core residues and establishes close contacts across both aromatic and hydrophobic components of the pocket (Ryosuke  et al.,2013). 

The interaction pattern was not identical for every ligand. Some ligands contacted aromatic residues through carbon atoms, whereas others contained oxygen or fluorine atoms positioned near aromatic residues. This demonstrates that the DBP can accommodate different chemical functionalities while maintaining occupation of the same structural region. 

Importantly, the automated analysis describes these interactions as “aromatic-contact candidates” based primarily on distance and atom/residue characteristics. They should not automatically be interpreted as experimentally confirmed π–π stacking, hydrogen bonding, halogen bonding, or other specific interaction types without examination of the complete 3D geometry. 

 

