Three of the four reference ligands reproduced at least five of the six core-residue contacts, while CHEMBL191703 reproduced four of six. CHEMBL4064721 and CHEMBL5569750 reproduced the complete 6/6 core-contact pattern. 

This provides structural support for the relevance of the defined six-residue DBP because experimentally characterized compounds were able to occupy the same region identified during the computational workflow (Ryosuke  et al.,2013). 

The reference-ligand results also demonstrate why docking score alone should not be used as the sole validation criterion. For example, CHEMBL297453 had a Vina score of −10.72 kcal/mol and five core contacts, whereas CHEMBL4064721 had a less negative score of −9.355 kcal/mol but six core contacts. Furthermore, the experimental activities are reported using different measures and units (MIC versus MEC), so a direct numerical correlation between the experimental activity values and Vina scores cannot be established from this small dataset. 

5.6 Overall interpretation 

Taken together, the docking and geometry analyses provide a consistent computational picture of the MexB DBP. The predicted ligands and experimentally characterized reference compounds were capable of occupying a common region defined by PHE178, VAL139, TYR327, PHE573, PHE610 and PHE628. 

The combination of P2Rank pocket prediction → Binding-state selection → DBP docking → core-residue interaction analysis → reference-ligand validation provides greater structural evidence than relying on docking affinity alone. 

The results therefore support the use of the identified six-residue region as a computationally defined DBP interaction signature for subsequent ligand prioritization. Experimental biochemical or microbiological assays would nevertheless be required to establish whether the predicted compounds inhibit MexB function and to determine their actual potency. 

6. Conclusion 

The present study established a structure-based computational workflow for characterization and ligand screening of the MexB multidrug efflux transporter. Structural analysis of the MexB trimer enabled identification of the Access, Binding and Extrusion conformational states, followed by selection of the Binding-state protomer for detailed investigation of ligand recognition (Ryosuke  et al.,2013). 

P2Rank-based pocket prediction and subsequent structural analysis identified a deep binding region containing six core residues: PHE178, VAL139, TYR327, PHE573, PHE610 and PHE628. These residues were subsequently used as a structural interaction signature for evaluating docked ligands (Radoslav  et al., 2018). 

Virtual screening and molecular docking identified five predicted ligands with Vina scores between −10.44 and −12.90 kcal/mol. All five predicted ligands established contacts within 4 Å of all six defined core residues. Among them, CHEMBL254328 produced the most negative docking score (−12.90 kcal/mol) and showed a complete 6/6 core-residue contact pattern, making it a suitable representative ligand for visualization and further computational investigation (Radoslav  et al., 2018). 

Independent docking of four experimentally characterized reference ligands provided additional structural validation. Three reference compounds reproduced five or all six core contacts, while the remaining compound reproduced four of six. This demonstrates that the computationally defined DBP is capable of accommodating experimentally characterized MexB-associated compounds (Ryosuke  et al.,2013). 

Overall, the study demonstrates that combining conformational-state analysis, P2Rank pocket prediction, AutoDock Vina docking, distance-based interaction analysis and reference-ligand validation can be used to characterize the MexB deep binding pocket and prioritize candidate ligands for further investigation.The computational results provide a structural basis for future studies aimed at developing MexB inhibitors. However, the docking scores and predicted interactions should be regarded as hypotheses requiring experimental validation, such as MexB inhibition assays, efflux assays, antimicrobial susceptibility testing, and ultimately biochemical or structural studies. 
